# Rank 1746 / CVE-2024-36042 (CWE-287): Silverpeas's authentication service
# selected its passwordless login path by asking whether the credential
# carried a password at all. AuthenticationCredential.isPasswordSet() is the
# null-check on the password field, documented in the vulnerable revision as
# "the password isn't required to participate in an authentication; for
# example in an NTLM negotiation" -- absence of the secret is the designed
# signal for "authenticated by an external mechanism". But the servlet fills
# that same field from request.getParameter("Password")
# (AuthenticationParameters, plain-login mode), so a login request that
# simply omits the Password parameter built a passwordless credential, and
# checkAuthentication routed it to authenticateByLoginAndDomain, whose whole
# contract is "authenticates the user only by its login and the domain" -- a
# user-table equality lookup followed by a session key. The password
# verification branch had always thrown on a null password; the dispatch just
# never sent a null password there. The fixed revision (11fb5e21) gives the
# credential an explicit `authenticated` boolean set only by
# setRemotelyAuthenticated() -- called solely in the SSO/CAS/internal-
# authtoken/social branches, where the server, not the request, established
# the identity -- flips checkAuthentication to dispatch on
# hasBeenRemotelyAuthenticated(), and renames the missing-password throw to
# AuthenticationPwdNotAvailException. Remediation confirmed present at
# 11fb5e21; the CredentialsServlet hunk is reformatting only.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked. The scan unit is now the three modules the chain
# crosses -- core-web/web/authentication, core-library/security/
# authentication and core-api/security/authentication together (99 java
# files), so the servlet-to-service call graph is visible; the first pass
# scanned the subtrees separately and no cross-module path could appear.
# Three --cache off runs per revision, full findings and review-flag views
# both: 48 findings each side, identical fingerprints except one VYQL-RF-002
# shifted by the CredentialsServlet reformat, and 38 against 36 review flags
# whose only difference is core.AuthenticationCheck on the three per-branch
# authService.authenticate calls versus the single hoisted one -- a control
# count that tracks the refactor, not the weakness. Nothing reports on the
# dispatch in either view.
#
# The taint route that answers this family now exists: the requested-mode
# accessor family (getAuthenticate, getAuthMode) labels its receiver
# code.ClientControlledSecurityDecision and VYQL-AUTH-098 reports the flow
# from client data, with provenance as the discriminator. A throwaway probe
# emitting that sink at the isPasswordSet call (at node and at
# callee.receiver, loading verified 33019 -> 33021 bindings) labels
# AuthenticationService.java:199, and no flow reaches either position:
# vyql trace -from code.HttpInput -to code.ClientControlledSecurityDecision
# reports the four request reads inside the AuthenticationParameters
# constructor (lines 98/99/102/103) with zero outgoing flow. A java object
# field store kills taint: the same request value carried in a local reaches
# a state-changing sink (VYQL-RF-003 fires) and reports nothing once stored
# on a field first -- same method, same sink, same rule (the pair below);
# the constructor-store-plus-getter and fluent-setter-plus-direct-read
# shapes are dead the same way. java.go's assignment_expression case lowers
# an identifier left side to nir.Assign but any field_access left side to a
# bare ExprStmt that evaluates and drops the value -- the java instance of
# the field-store family on record for go (no *ast.SelectorExpr store case
# beside the *ast.IndexExpr one) and php (member store reaches the element
# node but not the object), and wider than both: in java even the
# same-field read in the same method carries nothing, where the php keyed
# read still gets the element node. Not mechanical for a review pass: it
# adds flow edges to every java scan (every this.x = ... in every
# constructor and setter, 12 files across 7 campaign clones carry a direct
# this.x = request.getParameter(...) store) and needs the benchmark numbers
# a review pass cannot produce -- the same judgment the go half recorded.
# Engine-gap entry filed beside the ledger.
#
# The presence-route mirror key stays rejected, as measured in the first
# pass: a binding requiring the authenticator spellings plus isPasswordSet
# gives a clean 1 -> 0 across the revision pair and dies when isPasswordSet
# is renamed in the vulnerable file -- it detects this application's
# spelling, not the weakness; and the remediation's only trace inside the
# guarded method is vocabulary the fixing commit itself introduced
# (setRemotelyAuthenticated / hasBeenRemotelyAuthenticated), so a negation
# keyed on either encodes the fixed layout.
#
# Flip conditions: the first test flips to expect VYQL-AUTH-098 when a
# presence-predicate sink lands AND the field store carries taint (the
# decision then reads client-fed data at the vulnerable revision only -- the
# flagged dispatch reads a server-set boolean and stays reject). The pair's
# second test flips to expect VYQL-RF-003 as soon as the field store
# carries taint, independent of any authentication vocabulary.

test "rank1746: null password from an omitted request parameter selects the passwordless login path"
  lang java
  file core-library/src/main/java/org/silverpeas/core/security/authentication/AuthenticationService.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-001
  reject VYQL-AUTH-006
  reject VYQL-AUTH-098
  reject VYQL-AUTH-149
  code
  ```
  package org.silverpeas.core.security.authentication;

  public class AuthenticationService implements Authentication {

    public AuthenticationResponse authenticate(final AuthenticationCredential credential)
        throws AuthenticationException {
      final String key = checkAuthentication(credential);
      return AuthenticationResponse.succeed(key);
    }

    private String checkAuthentication(final AuthenticationCredential userCredential)
        throws AuthenticationException {
      final String key;
      if (userCredential.isPasswordSet()) {
        key = authenticateByLoginAndPasswordAndDomain(userCredential);
      } else {
        key = authenticateByLoginAndDomain(userCredential);
      }
      return key;
    }

    private String authenticateByLoginAndPasswordAndDomain(final AuthenticationCredential credential)
        throws AuthenticationException {
      String password = credential.getPassword();
      String domainId = credential.getDomainId();
      if (password == null || domainId == null) {
        throw new AuthenticationException("No login or no domain is specified!");
      }
      return getAuthenticationServer(domainId).authenticate(credential);
    }

    private String authenticateByLoginAndDomain(final AuthenticationCredential credential)
        throws AuthenticationException {
      String login = credential.getLogin();
      String domainId = credential.getDomainId();
      if (domainId == null) {
        throw new AuthenticationException("No domain is specified!");
      }
      final JdbcSqlQuery query = JdbcSqlQuery.select("id")
          .from("st_user")
          .where("domainId = ?", Integer.parseInt(domainId))
          .and("login = ?", login);
      return getAuthToken(credential);
    }
  }
  ```
